question:
A mathematician defines a function $f(x) = x^2 + bx + c$, where $b$ and $c$ are integers. She wants to find the values of $b$ and $c$ such that the system of equations 

\begin{align*}
f(p) - f(q) &= r, \\
p^2 - q^2 &= s
\end{align*}

has infinitely many solutions in real numbers $(p,q)$ for infinitely many pairs of integers $(r,s)$.  Find the product of all possible values of $b$.

solution:
We can think of the given expression as the sum of distances from $x$ to the integers from 1 to $n.$  To minimize this sum, we should take $x$ to be the average of 1 and $n$, which is $\frac{n + 1}{2}.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

int i;

draw((-1,0)--(12,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,8));

for (i = 1; i <= 11; ++i) {
  draw((i,-0.2)--(i,0.2));
}

label("$1$", (1,-0.2), S);
label("$2$", (2,-0.2), S);
label("$3$", (3,-0.2), S);
label("$n - 2$", (9,-0.2), S);
label("$n - 1$", (10,-0.2), S);
label("$n$", (11,-0.2), S);
label("$\frac{n + 1}{2}$", (6,0), S);

dot("$\frac{n + 1}{2}$", (6,0), S);
[/asy]

If $n$ is odd, then
\begin{align*}
|x - 1| + |x - 2| + |x - 3| + \dots + |x - n| &= \left( \frac{n - 1}{2} + \frac{n - 3}{2} + \dots + 2 + 1 \right) + \left( \frac{n - 1}{2} + \frac{n - 3}{2} + \dots + 2 + 1 \right) \\
&= 2 \cdot \frac{\frac{n - 1}{2} \cdot \frac{n + 1}{2}}{2} \\
&= \frac{(n - 1)(n + 1)}{4}.
\end{align*}

We want this to be at least 2023, so
\[\frac{(n - 1)(n + 1)}{4} \ge 2023.\]Then $(n - 1)(n + 1) \ge 8092.$  Since $90^2 = 8100$, $n = 91$ works.

If $n$ is even, then
\begin{align*}
|x - 1| + |x - 2| + |x - 3| + \dots + |x - n| &= \left( \frac{n}{2} + \frac{n - 2}{2} + \dots + 2 + 1 \right) + \left( \frac{n - 2}{2} + \frac{n - 4}{2} + \dots + 2 + 1 \right) \\
&= \frac{\frac{n}{2} \cdot \frac{n + 2}{2}}{2} + \frac{\frac{n - 2}{2} \cdot \frac{n}{2}}{2} \\
&= \frac{n(n + 2)}{8} + \frac{n(n - 2)}{8} \\
&= \frac{n^2}{4}.
\end{align*}

We want this to be at least 2023, so
\[\frac{n^2}{4} \ge 2023.\]Then $n^2 \ge 8092.$  Since $90^2 = 8100,$ $n = 90$ works.

The smaller of 90 and 91 is 90, so the smallest such $n$ is $\\boxed{90}$.

Let $(x,y)$ be the coordinates of the point.  Then $a = x$, $b = y$, $c = 10 - x$, and $d = 10 - y$.  Since $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ are integers, $x$ and $y$ must be integers.

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

int i;

draw((0,0)--(10,0));
draw((0,0)--(0,10));
draw((10,0)--(10,10));
draw((0,10)--(10,10));

for (i = 1; i <= 9; ++i) {
  draw((i,0)--(i,0.2));
  draw((0,i)--(0.2,i));
  draw((10,i)--(9.8,i));
  draw((i,10)--(i,9.8));
}

label("$x$", (10,0), E);
label("$y$", (0,10), N);

dot((3,7));
draw((3,7)--(3,0),dashed);
draw((3,7)--(0,7),dashed);
draw((3,7)--(10,7),dashed);
draw((3,7)--(3,10),dashed);

label("$a$", (3,3.5), E);
label("$b$", (1.5,7), N);
label("$c$", (6.5,7), N);
label("$d$", (3,8.5), E);
[/asy]

Furthermore, $a + c = 10$ and $b + d = 10.$  Thus, $a$ and $c$ must be a pair of positive integers that add up to 10, and $b$ and $d$ must be a pair of positive integers that add up to 10.  There are 9 such pairs: $(1,9),$ $(2,8)$, $\dots$, $(9,1)$.  This gives us $9 \times 9 = \\boxed{81}$ possible locations for the point.

question:
A sequence of squares is constructed as follows: The first square has side length 1.  To get the next square in the sequence, a square is drawn with one vertex at the center of the previous square, and its two adjacent vertices are a pair of adjacent vertices of the previous square, as shown below.  This process is repeated infinitely.  Find the sum of the areas of all the squares.

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, P, Q, R, S;

A = (0,0);
B = (1,0);
C = (1,1);
D = (0,1);
P = (A + C)/2;
Q = extension(A, B, P, P + dir(135));
R = extension(B, C, P, P + dir(45));
S = extension(C, D, P, P + dir(-45));

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(Q--R--S--cycle);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, NE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
[/asy]
